                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran revisits the long-running debate over how rising interest rates affect equity prices, arguing the relationship is not mechanical or universal. With US Treasury rates at 20-year highs in 2026, equity markets have remained resilient because strong corporate earnings growth has offset higher discount rates. He demonstrates that rate impact varies significantly by sector: technology and energy have outperformed, while consumer and utilities sectors have lagged.",
                                                              "take":  "Damodaran is doing what he does best: dismantling a market truism. The reflexive \u0027rates up, stocks down\u0027 narrative has been wrong twice in the past decade, and this post is a useful reminder that the real variable is whether cash flows are growing fast enough to outrun the discount rate headwind. For investors, the implication is actionable: stop watching the Fed pivot and start watching earnings revisions. Sectors with pricing power and durable growth ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â tech, energy ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â are structurally better positioned in a high-rate world than bond proxies. The market already knows this; Damodaran is just showing the math.",
