                                                              "summary":  "Josh Brown argues that Wall Street\u0027s concern about data center overbuilding fundamentally misreads the scale of compute demand that\u0027s coming. The real use case ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a world with a billion personal AI assistants, autonomous systems, and robots ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â dwarfs anything current chatbots and coding tools require. Brown contends that tech insiders and operators see this future clearly, while investors anchored to today\u0027s AI applications are systematically underestimating infrastructure needs.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown is making a conviction call that the AI capex skeptics are looking at the wrong time horizon ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â and he\u0027s probably right that demand will eventually be enormous. But \u0027eventually\u0027 is doing a lot of work here. The critical risk he underweights is timing: if that billion-agent future is 5-7 years away, today\u0027s data center overbuilding creates a real valley of oversupply that will punish hyperscaler margins and chip names in the interim. The bull case is real; the question is whether investors can stomach the air pocket between here and there. This is a long-duration bet, and Brown is pitching it as if the timeline is obvious when it is not.",
