                                                              "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron, founder of Baron Capital, whose fund has been the top-performing US mutual fund since 2003 by delivering over $60 billion in returns. The core of Baron\u0027s edge is extreme conviction holding ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â he bought Tesla at $14/share in 2014 and still holds a multi-billion position, and has an estimated $25 billion stake in SpaceX. Baron\u0027s philosophy is to identify visionary founders and hold through volatility rather than trading around positions.",
                                                              "take":  "The Baron profile is a useful corrective to the short-termism endemic to active management. His returns are essentially the product of one or two massive, long-duration bets made with conviction most funds cannot institutionally sustain. The lesson is not simply \u0027find the next Tesla\u0027; it is that the structural conditions enabling Baron\u0027s approach ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â closed-end capital, aligned fee structure, founder control ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â matter as much as stock-picking skill. Most retail investors and fund managers cannot replicate this without changing how they are capitalized. The takeaway is less about Baron\u0027s genius and more about designing the right incentive structures.",
